# Explicit supervision integration

This opt-in supports only the full SQLite LMS. A stable CLI pins the exact
matching registry package; a prerelease CLI requires an explicitly selected
matching local source. Neither mode establishes a deployment, verified family
relationship or legal compliance.

Before startup, configure `RULLST_SUPERVISION_DATABASE` as a private absolute file
path in a trusted directory, `RULLST_SUPERVISION_EPOCH` as an independently retained
opaque deployment identifier, and `RULLST_SUPERVISION_FORM_KEY_HEX` as a separate
random nonzero 32-byte hexadecimal secret. Store it in your secret manager; do not
commit it. All processes must use the same configuration. No automatic database
creation or recovery occurs at startup.

Run `cargo run --bin supervision-admin -- /private/command.json` locally as a
trusted operator. Each bounded JSON command has this shape:

```json
{"tenant":"school-1","operator":"operator-7","evidence":"case-42","command":{"operation":"initialize"}}
```

Initialization exclusively creates a new file and grants no authority. The
operator/evidence fields are audit references, not authentication. The local OS
operator must independently verify identities, current school membership,
relationship, legal basis and purpose before granting authority. Never expose
this executable or an unchecked `Operator` constructor through a web endpoint.
No documents, biometrics or relationship evidence belong in this file/store.

Other `command` objects:

- `{"operation":"enroll","subject":1}` enables management, initially denying
  protected learning. Perform this before exposing the learner's application.
- `{"operation":"grant","subject":1,"delegate":2,"action":"parental_manage","expires_at":<future UTC epoch seconds>}`
  grants management of the fixed `learning` resource. First creation omits
  `revision`; replacement requires the current revision.
- `{"operation":"grant","subject":1,"delegate":3,"action":"exam_review","lesson":1,"expires_at":<future UTC epoch seconds>}`
  grants separate review for that learner/lesson. A parental grant never implies it.
- `inspect_grant` uses the same subject/delegate/action/optional lesson fields.
- `revoke` uses those fields plus the exact current `revision`. Revoking a manager
  preserves the learner's existing restrictions.
- `{"operation":"remove","subject":1,"revision":<current policy revision>}`
  explicitly removes management; obtain the latest revision from the authorized
  parental form. Ordinary enrollment and school authorization remain mandatory.
- `{"operation":"purge","limit":100}` removes at most 100 expired rows for this
  tenant. Schedule bounded cleanup; repeatedly call if a backlog remains.

Example routes (replace IDs with existing authorized records):

- Learner: `/supervision/exam?school=school-1&lesson=1`. Starting redirects to an
  opaque session URL. Opening the start page again recovers the current active
  or paused session. A start form from before the last session was ended cannot
  start another session: reload to obtain its new revision.
- Learner/manager: `/supervision/parental?school=school-1&subject=1`.
- Reviewer: `/supervision/review?school=school-1&subject=1&lesson=1&session=<id>`.
  The operator/learner supplies this URL to the authorized reviewer separately.

All requests refresh authenticated school membership. Subject membership and
lesson/course school binding are checked separately. Missing/expired/revoked
grants deny delegated operations. Existing lesson, progress and quiz service
calls pass through the added parental gate. This is an additional restriction,
not an enrollment grant; unmanaged learners retain ordinary authorization.
Only server code selects the `learning` policy resource. Existing authorized
cross-subject administrator corrections retain their original authorization and
require current actor/subject school membership, without applying learner-use
restrictions. They grant no exam-review or parental-management permission. Existing learning routes
use their original authenticated school-selection rules; supervision's `school`
query hint does not alter unrelated routes.

Own session state and pause/end controls remain available after learning access
is restricted, provided current school membership and lesson binding still hold.
Starting, resuming and reporting require current learning access. Paused/ended
states cannot accept reports. Forms bind actor, cookie session, school, learner,
resource, configured categories, policy, notice and revision and expire after five minutes. A failed or
cancelled write may have committed: reload state before retrying.

Only the selected categories on this page are reported: visibility by default;
`--browser-observations visibility,focus,clipboard,fullscreen` selects all four
at generation time. Start/resume acknowledgement binds exact categories; browser
permission for camera/audio is separate and never requested by this generator.
Copy/cut/paste reports contain no clipboard contents. Focus cannot identify other
windows, and fullscreen events do not force fullscreen or establish integrity.
Reports are limited to at most one per second,
256 per session and 8192 stored across all tenants. Dropped/forged reports are
expected; they never establish misconduct or change grades. Forms work without
JavaScript, with no browser reports. Submission of pause/end stops local
collection immediately; the server transition must still succeed. A different
tab can attempt a report until it learns of the change, but committed state
changes reject it. The collector serializes requests with at most sixteen queued occurrences;
overflow stops collection and clears the queue. There are no background
heartbeats or automatic renewal.
Five-minute form expiry stops local collection until the page is reloaded.

The configured event retention and maximum session lifetime are shown to the
learner. Reads exclude expired records before scheduled purge. Session metadata
remains for the lifetime plus retention; logical deletion does not erase WAL,
free pages or backups. Grants/managed learners/sessions have global capacities
1024/512/256 and are never silently evicted. Exhaustion/storage/clock/configuration
failures deny protected operations. Changing persisted limits requires a reviewed
new store, not editing startup constants against an existing one.

This is local shared-file state, not multi-host replication. App membership and
supervision use separate database transactions. Operators own private filesystem
access, encryption, backup retention and trustworthy clocks. Restore with the
feature quiesced and a new independently retained epoch; restoring an old file
with its old epoch can restore revoked authority. No automatic rollback detection,
OS/device control, recurring schedules, screen-time accounting or camera analysis
is provided. Review course policies and relationship evidence periodically.

The unpublished observation extension uses schema v2. Schema v1 is refused;
there is no automatic migration, acknowledgement upgrade or deletion. Quiesce
the old preview, retain/expire its state under the existing retention policy and
prepare a separately named private v2 store with a new independently retained
epoch. Independently re-establish grants/restrictions before allowing protected
learning; a fresh store has no enrolled parental restrictions. Do not just change
the epoch against an old file or delete state to bypass the refusal.
